//! The GL module: OpenGL, with the library **chosen and linked per operating system** at compile time.
//!
//! ADR-0184 §5 — the first module whose *declarations* are generated rather than written, and the reason
//! Wave A exists. OpenGL is the case that could not be bound at all before it:
//!
//! | Target | Link form | Why |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | macOS | `-framework OpenGL` | it is a **framework**, and `-lOpenGL` does not resolve — measured |
//! | Linux | `-lGL` | an ordinary shared object |
//! | Windows | `-lopengl32` | a different name again |
//!
//! Three names *and* two argument forms, where `modules/Window`'s SDL2 is one name everywhere. That is
//! precisely why the graphics stack was built on SDL2 first (ADR-0182 §2) and why this module needed two
//! compiler changes before a line of it could be written:
//!
//!   * `jr-link` could emit only `-L` and `-l` (ADR-0183). It emits `-framework` now.
//!   * `#insert` was not a file-scope directive, so comptime code could generate *statements* and not
//!     *declarations* (ADR-0184). It can now.
//!
//! # How the declaration is chosen
//!
//! ```text
//! #insert #run gl_library();
//! ```
//!
//! `gl_library` is ordinary Jairs, run at compile time, returning the text of one declaration. There is no
//! `#if` in this language and none was added: `os()` is a compile-time **value** (ADR-0180), so choosing
//! between declarations is choosing between strings.
//!
//! **The generated text contains a string *literal*, and that is load-bearing.** The Simp plan concluded a
//! per-OS library name was unreachable because `#system_library` needs a bare literal and a computed
//! operand cannot be evaluated before the library must be known — a real cycle. Generating the declaration
//! side-steps it entirely: by the time the compiler resolves this library, the operand *is* a literal
//! (ADR-0184 §4).
//!
//! # What is bound, and what is not
//!
//! **OpenGL 1.1 only.** Every entry point below is in the 1.1 core, which means it is exported by the
//! library itself on all three targets and needs no loader. Anything later — shaders, VBOs, framebuffers —
//! is reached on Windows through `wglGetProcAddress` rather than by linking, so a `#foreign` declaration of
//! `glCreateShader` links on macOS and Linux and fails on Windows. That is a **loader**, it is its own
//! wave, and `SDL_GL_GetProcAddress` is the portable way to write it — which keeps SDL2 as the platform
//! layer exactly as Jai's `Simp` keeps `Window_Creation`.
//!
//! **No context creation.** A GL call needs a current context, and creating one is per-OS window-system
//! work (`CGL`, `GLX`, `WGL`) or one SDL2 call. `Window`/`Simp` own the window, so context creation belongs
//! there when a GL backend is written (ADR-0182 §6 left that as a later swap behind an unchanged API).
//!
//! **Every call here is undefined without a current context**, and that is not a Jairs limitation but
//! OpenGL's contract. `glGetString` with no context **segfaults** on macOS rather than returning null —
//! measured while writing this module. So nothing here is called by this module's own test; what the test
//! asserts is that the symbols *linked*, which is the claim this module makes.

#import "Basic";

// The library declaration, chosen per OS at compile time. This is the whole per-OS surface of the module:
// every binding below names `gl` and does not care which of the three it turned out to be.
#insert #run gl_library();

/// `GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT`, for `clear`.
COLOR_BUFFER_BIT :: 16384;

/// `GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT`.
DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT :: 256;

/// `GL_VERSION`, for `version_string`.
VERSION :: 7938;

/// `GL_VENDOR`.
VENDOR :: 7936;

/// `GL_RENDERER`.
RENDERER :: 7937;

/// `GL_NO_ERROR` — what `error_code` returns when nothing has gone wrong.
NO_ERROR :: 0;

/// Sets the colour `clear` fills with, each channel 0..1.
///
/// Floats in 0..1, matching both OpenGL's own signature and `Simp`'s convention (ADR-0182 §3) — so a
/// caller moving between the two renderers does not change numeric range.
set_clear_color :: (r: float32, g: float32, b: float32, a: float32) {
    gl_clear_color(r, g, b, a);
}

/// Clears the buffers named by `mask` — `COLOR_BUFFER_BIT`, or it and `DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT` together.
clear :: (mask: s64) {
    gl_clear(cast(u32, mask));
}

/// Sets the viewport, in pixels from the lower-left.
///
/// **Lower-left, not upper-left**: OpenGL's origin is the opposite of `Simp`'s and of every window
/// system's. Stated because it is the single most common source of an upside-down first frame, and a
/// wrapper that silently flipped it would be lying about which API a reader is using.
set_viewport :: (x: s64, y: s64, width: s64, height: s64) {
    gl_viewport(cast(s32, x), cast(s32, y), cast(s32, width), cast(s32, height));
}

/// The last error, or `NO_ERROR`.
///
/// OpenGL's errors are a *queue* a caller drains, so one call reports one error and clears it. Not `#must`:
/// `NO_ERROR` is the answer on every well-behaved call and a marker there would make every frame carry a
/// ceremony for a non-event — ADR-0151's line.
error_code :: () -> s64 {
    return cast(s64, gl_get_error());
}

/// One of the driver's descriptive strings — pass `VERSION`, `VENDOR` or `RENDERER`.
///
/// A NUL-terminated `*u8` the driver owns, valid for the life of the context. **Null, or a crash, with no
/// current context**: on macOS this segfaults rather than returning null, which is OpenGL's contract and
/// not something this module can guard.
version_string :: (name: s64) -> *u8 {
    return gl_get_string(cast(u32, name));
}

/// Blocks until every issued command has completed.
finish :: () {
    gl_finish();
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shaders, buffers and drawing — what a real renderer needs (ADR-0187 §4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **Every constant below is a `GLenum`, and every wrapper takes an `s64` and casts.** A Jairs constant is
// `s64` and there is no syntax to annotate one narrower — `VERTEX_SHADER : u32 : 35633` does not parse,
// probed. So the narrowing happens once, here, rather than at every call site: that is the same
// discipline `modules/Window` argues for at its own SDL boundary, and it is why a typed constant is the
// highest-value small language item this library still wants.

/// `GL_VERTEX_SHADER`.
VERTEX_SHADER :: 35633;

/// `GL_FRAGMENT_SHADER`.
FRAGMENT_SHADER :: 35632;

/// `GL_BLEND`, for `enable`/`disable`.
BLEND :: 3042;

/// `GL_SRC_ALPHA`, a blend factor.
SRC_ALPHA :: 770;

/// `GL_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA`, a blend factor.
ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA :: 771;

/// `GL_TEXTURE_2D`.
TEXTURE_2D :: 3553;

/// `GL_RGBA`, a texture format.
RGBA :: 6408;

/// `GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE`, a texture component type.
UNSIGNED_BYTE :: 5121;

/// `GL_LINEAR`, a texture filter.
LINEAR :: 9729;

/// Creates a shader of `kind` — `VERTEX_SHADER` or `FRAGMENT_SHADER`. Zero on failure.
create_shader :: (kind: s64) -> u32 {
    return gl_create_shader(cast(u32, kind));
}

/// Hands `source` to `shader`, **with its length**.
///
/// The length is passed explicitly and this is the whole reason the wrapper exists. `glShaderSource` with
/// a null length array reads each string until a **NUL**, and a Jairs `string` is `{data, count}` with no
/// terminator — so the null form compiles the shader from whatever follows the text in memory, reports
/// `GL_COMPILE_STATUS` of 0, and leaves `glGetError` saying `GL_NO_ERROR`. A silent failure with a clean
/// error code, measured while writing `modules/Simp`.
///
/// Returns `false` for an empty source, which GL accepts and which is never what a caller meant.
set_shader_source :: (shader: u32, source: string) -> bool {
    if source.count == 0 {
        return false;
    }
    text := source.data;
    length := cast(s32, source.count);
    gl_shader_source(shader, 1, *text, *length);
    return true;
}

/// Compiles a shader whose source is set.
compile_shader :: (shader: u32) {
    gl_compile_shader(shader);
}

/// Whether `shader` compiled.
///
/// A predicate rather than an out-parameter, because `GL_COMPILE_STATUS` has exactly two useful values
/// and a caller that wants the log asks `shader_log`.
shader_compiled :: (shader: u32) -> bool {
    status: s32 = 0;
    gl_get_shader_iv(shader, cast(u32, COMPILE_STATUS), *status);
    return status != 0;
}

/// The driver's compile log for `shader`, written into `buffer` and returned as a view.
///
/// The caller owns the buffer, so this module allocates nothing and the log is valid as long as the
/// buffer is. An empty view means the driver had nothing to say, which is normal on success.
shader_log :: (shader: u32, buffer: []u8) -> []u8 {
    if buffer.count == 0 {
        return buffer;
    }
    // A view exposes `.count` but no `.data`, so the base pointer comes from the address of element
    // zero — which is the same address, and is guarded by the emptiness check above.
    base := *buffer[0];
    written: s32 = 0;
    gl_get_shader_info_log(shader, cast(s32, buffer.count), *written, base);
    return view(base, cast(s64, written));
}

/// Creates a program. Zero on failure.
create_program :: () -> u32 {
    return gl_create_program();
}

/// Attaches a compiled shader to a program.
attach_shader :: (program: u32, shader: u32) {
    gl_attach_shader(program, shader);
}

/// Links a program whose shaders are attached.
link_program :: (program: u32) {
    gl_link_program(program);
}

/// Whether `program` linked.
program_linked :: (program: u32) -> bool {
    status: s32 = 0;
    gl_get_program_iv(program, cast(u32, LINK_STATUS), *status);
    return status != 0;
}

/// Makes `program` current.
use_program :: (program: u32) {
    gl_use_program(program);
}

/// The location of a uniform by name, or `-1` when the program has no such uniform.
///
/// The name is NUL-terminated into temporary storage, for the reason `set_shader_source` explains about
/// lengths: GL reads a uniform name to a NUL and a Jairs `string` has none.
uniform_location :: (program: u32, name: string) -> s32 {
    c_name, ok := nul_terminated(name);
    if !ok {
        return -1;
    }
    return gl_get_uniform_location(program, c_name);
}

/// Sets an `int` uniform.
set_uniform_int :: (location: s32, value: s64) {
    gl_uniform_1i(location, cast(s32, value));
}

/// Sets a `mat4` uniform from 16 floats in **column-major** order.
///
/// `transpose = false`, which is what column-major means to GL. Stated because passing row-major data
/// with `false` produces a picture that is wrong in a way that looks like a coordinate-system bug.
set_uniform_matrix4 :: (location: s32, values: *float32) {
    gl_uniform_matrix4fv(location, 1, cast(u8, 0), values);
}

/// Creates a buffer object. Zero on failure.
create_buffer :: () -> u32 {
    name: u32 = 0;
    gl_gen_buffers(1, *name);
    return name;
}

/// Binds a buffer as the array buffer.
bind_array_buffer :: (buffer: u32) {
    gl_bind_buffer(cast(u32, ARRAY_BUFFER), buffer);
}

/// Uploads `bytes` from `data` into the bound array buffer, replacing its contents.
///
/// `GL_STREAM_DRAW`, because a batch is written once and drawn once — which is what an immediate-mode
/// renderer does every frame, and telling the driver so is free.
///
/// `data` is a `*u8` and not a typed pointer, deliberately. A first attempt gave it a named
/// one-field struct so the type would say "vertex data" — and that is *worse* than `*u8` here, because
/// the caller's element type is its own `Vertex`, declared in another module, so every call site would
/// need a cast between two unrelated struct types. A byte pointer is what `glBufferData` takes and what
/// the caller can produce; the meaning of the bytes belongs to the caller's vertex layout, which this
/// module cannot check anyway.
upload_array_buffer :: (bytes: s64, data: *u8) {
    gl_buffer_data(cast(u32, ARRAY_BUFFER), bytes, data, cast(u32, STREAM_DRAW));
}

/// Enables a float vertex attribute at `index`, reading `components` floats every `stride` bytes.
///
/// `normalised = false`: these are real coordinates and real 0..1 colours, not integers to be scaled.
enable_vertex_attribute :: (index: s64, components: s64, stride: s64, offset: s64) {
    gl_enable_vertex_attrib_array(cast(u32, index));
    gl_vertex_attrib_pointer(cast(u32, index), cast(s32, components), cast(u32, FLOAT), cast(u8, 0), cast(s32, stride), offset);
}

/// Disables the vertex attribute at `index`.
disable_vertex_attribute :: (index: s64) {
    gl_disable_vertex_attrib_array(cast(u32, index));
}

/// Draws `count` vertices as triangles from the bound array buffer.
draw_triangles :: (count: s64) {
    gl_draw_arrays(cast(u32, TRIANGLES), 0, cast(s32, count));
}

/// Enables a capability — pass `BLEND`.
enable :: (capability: s64) {
    gl_enable(cast(u32, capability));
}

/// Disables a capability.
disable :: (capability: s64) {
    gl_disable(cast(u32, capability));
}

/// Sets the blend factors.
set_blend_func :: (source: s64, destination: s64) {
    gl_blend_func(cast(u32, source), cast(u32, destination));
}

/// Creates a texture object. Zero on failure.
create_texture :: () -> u32 {
    name: u32 = 0;
    gl_gen_textures(1, *name);
    return name;
}

/// Binds `texture` to texture unit 0 as a 2D texture.
///
/// Unit 0 only, because this library's renderer samples one texture at a time. A multi-texture path would
/// take the unit as an argument, and nothing needs it yet.
bind_texture_unit_0 :: (texture: u32) {
    gl_active_texture(cast(u32, TEXTURE0));
    gl_bind_texture(cast(u32, TEXTURE_2D), texture);
}

/// Deletes a texture object.
///
/// Safe on zero, which GL defines as a no-op — so a caller can delete on every path without tracking
/// whether the texture was ever created, the property `File.close` has for the same reason.
delete_texture :: (texture: u32) {
    if texture == 0 {
        return;
    }
    name := texture;
    gl_delete_textures(1, *name);
}

/// Sets how many bytes a row of uploaded pixel data is aligned to.
///
/// GL defaults to **4**, and an RGBA image is always a multiple of 4 bytes per pixel, so the default is
/// right for `upload_texture_rgba`. It is exposed because a *three*-channel image is not: a 3-pixel-wide
/// RGB row is 9 bytes, GL reads 12, and the picture skews progressively — the classic symptom. A caller
/// uploading anything but tightly-packed RGBA sets this to 1 first.
set_unpack_alignment :: (bytes: s64) {
    gl_pixel_store_i(cast(u32, UNPACK_ALIGNMENT), cast(s32, bytes));
}

/// Uploads RGBA pixels into the bound 2D texture and sets linear filtering.
///
/// Linear rather than nearest, matching Jai's `Simp` default for a loaded image. `pixels` is
/// `width * height * 4` bytes, tightly packed.
upload_texture_rgba :: (width: s64, height: s64, pixels: *u8) {
    gl_tex_image_2d(cast(u32, TEXTURE_2D), 0, cast(s32, RGBA), cast(s32, width), cast(s32, height), 0, cast(u32, RGBA), cast(u32, UNSIGNED_BYTE), pixels);
    gl_tex_parameter_i(cast(u32, TEXTURE_2D), cast(u32, TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER), cast(s32, LINEAR));
    gl_tex_parameter_i(cast(u32, TEXTURE_2D), cast(u32, TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER), cast(s32, LINEAR));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The per-OS library choice — exported, then the raw bindings, which are not
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `gl_library_for` sits **above** `#scope_module` deliberately, and it is the only thing here that does. The
// per-OS mapping is the claim this module makes about three platforms, and a claim no test can reach is not
// one — see its own docs for why it takes the OS rather than reading it. Everything below the marker is
// private: the raw `#foreign` bindings are the module's implementation, and ADR-0179 §6's rule is that a
// module's exported names are a claim on every importer's flat namespace.

/// The text of a given platform's OpenGL library declaration (ADR-0184 §5).
///
/// Run at compile time by the `#insert` above. Three targets, **two different linker arguments**, and the
/// difference is why `jr-link` needed a second form at all (ADR-0183 §1):
///
///   * macOS ships OpenGL as a *framework*, so `-lOpenGL` fails with `ld: library 'OpenGL' not found`
///     while `-framework OpenGL` links. Measured, not assumed.
///   * Linux has `libGL.so`, an ordinary `-lGL`.
///   * Windows has `opengl32.lib`, a different name and the ordinary form.
///
/// **Windows is unverified.** `-lopengl32` is what a Unix-shaped driver would emit and whether `clang` on
/// Windows resolves it to `opengl32.lib` has never been tried — PLAN records it. The declaration is right
/// on paper and the link is not evidence yet.
///
/// A procedure returning text rather than a `#if`, because `os()` is a compile-time *value* and this
/// language has no item-level conditional — deliberately, since generating the declaration is strictly more
/// general and needed no new syntax (ADR-0184 §1).
///
/// **It takes the OS rather than reading `os()` itself, so that every branch is reachable on any host.** That
/// is the whole reason for the split with `gl_library` below, and it is not a stylistic one: a generator that
/// reads `os()` internally has exactly *one* executable path per machine, so the Linux and Windows branches
/// of this module would be text no test could ever look at. Two of three branches permanently unexecuted is
/// not a portability claim, it is a hope. Parameterised, `tests/corpus/valid/137-per-os-library-text.jr`
/// asserts all three strings on whatever host runs it — and asserts they are *different*, which is what
/// catches the mistake a reader would actually make here: editing one branch and leaving another aliased to
/// the same text.
gl_library_for :: (target: Operating_System) -> string {
    if target == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return "gl :: #framework \"OpenGL\";";
    }
    if target == Operating_System.LINUX {
        return "gl :: #system_library \"GL\";";
    }
    return "gl :: #system_library \"opengl32\";";
}

/// This host's declaration — `gl_library_for` applied to `os()`.
///
/// The `#insert` calls *this*, so the module still picks its own library and nothing about the compile-time
/// path changed. It exists only to keep the choice (`os()`) apart from the mapping (which text per OS), so
/// the mapping is testable.
gl_library :: () -> string {
    return gl_library_for(os());
}

#scope_module

// The private constants: values a caller never names, so they stay behind `#scope_module` where a
// module's exported names are a claim on every importer's flat namespace (ADR-0179 §6).

/// `GL_COMPILE_STATUS`.
COMPILE_STATUS :: 35713;

/// `GL_LINK_STATUS`.
LINK_STATUS :: 35714;

/// `GL_ARRAY_BUFFER`.
ARRAY_BUFFER :: 34962;

/// `GL_STREAM_DRAW`.
STREAM_DRAW :: 35040;

/// `GL_FLOAT`.
FLOAT :: 5126;

/// `GL_TRIANGLES`.
TRIANGLES :: 4;

/// `GL_TEXTURE0`.
TEXTURE0 :: 33984;

/// `GL_TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER`.
TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER :: 10241;

/// `GL_TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER`.
TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER :: 10240;

/// Copies `text` into NUL-terminated scratch from temporary storage.
///
/// The same helper `File` and `Window` keep private for the same reason: the pointer lives only until the
/// next `reset_temporary_storage`, and GL copies a uniform name during the call.
nul_terminated :: (text: string) -> (*u8, bool) {
    scratch := talloc(text.count + 1);
    if scratch == null {
        return null, false;
    }
    bytes := view(scratch, text.count + 1);
    i := 0;
    while i < text.count {
        bytes[i] = cast(u8, (text.data + i).*);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    bytes[text.count] = cast(u8, 0);
    return scratch, true;
}

/// `glCreateShader(GLenum type)` -> a shader name.
gl_create_shader :: (kind: u32) -> u32 #foreign gl "glCreateShader";

/// `glShaderSource(GLuint, GLsizei, const GLchar **, const GLint *)`.
gl_shader_source :: (shader: u32, count: s32, strings: **u8, lengths: *s32) #foreign gl "glShaderSource";

/// `glCompileShader(GLuint)`.
gl_compile_shader :: (shader: u32) #foreign gl "glCompileShader";

/// `glGetShaderiv(GLuint, GLenum, GLint *)`.
gl_get_shader_iv :: (shader: u32, parameter: u32, out: *s32) #foreign gl "glGetShaderiv";

/// `glGetShaderInfoLog(GLuint, GLsizei, GLsizei *, GLchar *)`.
gl_get_shader_info_log :: (shader: u32, capacity: s32, written: *s32, log: *u8) #foreign gl "glGetShaderInfoLog";

/// `glCreateProgram()` -> a program name.
gl_create_program :: () -> u32 #foreign gl "glCreateProgram";

/// `glAttachShader(GLuint program, GLuint shader)`.
gl_attach_shader :: (program: u32, shader: u32) #foreign gl "glAttachShader";

/// `glLinkProgram(GLuint)`.
gl_link_program :: (program: u32) #foreign gl "glLinkProgram";

/// `glGetProgramiv(GLuint, GLenum, GLint *)`.
gl_get_program_iv :: (program: u32, parameter: u32, out: *s32) #foreign gl "glGetProgramiv";

/// `glUseProgram(GLuint)`.
gl_use_program :: (program: u32) #foreign gl "glUseProgram";

/// `glGetUniformLocation(GLuint, const GLchar *)` -> a location, or `-1`.
gl_get_uniform_location :: (program: u32, name: *u8) -> s32 #foreign gl "glGetUniformLocation";

/// `glUniform1i(GLint, GLint)`.
gl_uniform_1i :: (location: s32, value: s32) #foreign gl "glUniform1i";

/// `glUniformMatrix4fv(GLint, GLsizei, GLboolean, const GLfloat *)`.
gl_uniform_matrix4fv :: (location: s32, count: s32, transpose: u8, values: *float32) #foreign gl "glUniformMatrix4fv";

/// `glGenBuffers(GLsizei, GLuint *)`.
gl_gen_buffers :: (count: s32, names: *u32) #foreign gl "glGenBuffers";

/// `glBindBuffer(GLenum, GLuint)`.
gl_bind_buffer :: (target: u32, buffer: u32) #foreign gl "glBindBuffer";

/// `glBufferData(GLenum, GLsizeiptr, const void *, GLenum)`.
gl_buffer_data :: (target: u32, size: s64, data: *u8, usage: u32) #foreign gl "glBufferData";

/// `glEnableVertexAttribArray(GLuint)`.
gl_enable_vertex_attrib_array :: (index: u32) #foreign gl "glEnableVertexAttribArray";

/// `glDisableVertexAttribArray(GLuint)`.
gl_disable_vertex_attrib_array :: (index: u32) #foreign gl "glDisableVertexAttribArray";

/// `glVertexAttribPointer(GLuint, GLint, GLenum, GLboolean, GLsizei, const void *)`.
///
/// The last argument is a **byte offset** into the bound buffer, not a pointer, which is why it is an
/// `s64` here rather than a `*u8`: GL reinterprets an integer as an offset when a buffer is bound, and a
/// pointer type would invite a caller to pass an address.
gl_vertex_attrib_pointer :: (index: u32, size: s32, kind: u32, normalised: u8, stride: s32, offset: s64) #foreign gl "glVertexAttribPointer";

/// `glDrawArrays(GLenum, GLint, GLsizei)`.
gl_draw_arrays :: (mode: u32, first: s32, count: s32) #foreign gl "glDrawArrays";

/// `glEnable(GLenum)`.
gl_enable :: (capability: u32) #foreign gl "glEnable";

/// `glDisable(GLenum)`.
gl_disable :: (capability: u32) #foreign gl "glDisable";

/// `glBlendFunc(GLenum, GLenum)`.
gl_blend_func :: (source: u32, destination: u32) #foreign gl "glBlendFunc";

/// `glGenTextures(GLsizei, GLuint *)`.
gl_gen_textures :: (count: s32, names: *u32) #foreign gl "glGenTextures";

/// `glActiveTexture(GLenum)`.
gl_active_texture :: (unit: u32) #foreign gl "glActiveTexture";

/// `glBindTexture(GLenum, GLuint)`.
gl_bind_texture :: (target: u32, texture: u32) #foreign gl "glBindTexture";

/// `glTexImage2D(GLenum, GLint, GLint, GLsizei, GLsizei, GLint, GLenum, GLenum, const void *)`.
gl_tex_image_2d :: (target: u32, level: s32, internal_format: s32, width: s32, height: s32, border: s32, format: u32, kind: u32, pixels: *u8) #foreign gl "glTexImage2D";

/// `GL_UNPACK_ALIGNMENT`.
UNPACK_ALIGNMENT :: 3317;

/// `glDeleteTextures(GLsizei, const GLuint *)`.
gl_delete_textures :: (count: s32, names: *u32) #foreign gl "glDeleteTextures";

/// `glPixelStorei(GLenum, GLint)`.
gl_pixel_store_i :: (parameter: u32, value: s32) #foreign gl "glPixelStorei";

/// `glTexParameteri(GLenum, GLenum, GLint)`.
gl_tex_parameter_i :: (target: u32, parameter: u32, value: s32) #foreign gl "glTexParameteri";

/// `glClearColor(GLclampf red, GLclampf green, GLclampf blue, GLclampf alpha)`.
///
/// `GLclampf` is a C `float`, so these are `float32` — the width matters at a C boundary for the reason
/// `Window`'s own `Rect` docs give at length (ADR-0182 §2).
gl_clear_color :: (r: float32, g: float32, b: float32, a: float32) #foreign gl "glClearColor";

/// `glClear(GLbitfield mask)` — `GLbitfield` is a `Uint32`.
gl_clear :: (mask: u32) #foreign gl "glClear";

/// `glViewport(GLint x, GLint y, GLsizei width, GLsizei height)` — four C `int`s.
gl_viewport :: (x: s32, y: s32, width: s32, height: s32) #foreign gl "glViewport";

/// `glGetError()` -> `GLenum`, a `Uint32`.
gl_get_error :: () -> u32 #foreign gl "glGetError";

/// `glGetString(GLenum name)` -> `const GLubyte *`.
gl_get_string :: (name: u32) -> *u8 #foreign gl "glGetString";

/// `glFinish()`.
gl_finish :: () #foreign gl "glFinish";
